  "slug": "protein-design-631c",
  "summary": "Five preprints probe how evolutionary inputs, benchmarks and optimization constraints determine ML-guided protein engineering outcomes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is making computational protein design choices explicit and testable. SCOPE integrates structure and evolution in residue-level graph learning to predict enzyme catalytic residues, with mutagenesis validation. A separate study treats MSAs as tunable inputs rather than fixed artifacts, showing that evolutionary information shapes protein generation and folding. A benchmark of 557,143 simulated ML-guided directed evolution campaigns across 16 landscapes finds that design choices transfer poorly between landscapes. GRACE addresses discrete sequence optimization for protein, DNA and RNA under competing constraints using gradients and augmented Lagrangian methods. Structure-guided mining of Zn-His scaffolds combined with small-sample machine learning predicts acetophenone reduction activity. Collectively, the emphasis is on input curation, generalizability and constraint handling, supported by experimental assays in some cases.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
